In article <laaksone.33.00C174C1@csc.fi>, laaksone@csc.fi (Leif Laaksonen) 
writes:

|>I have a problem. I used to combine Fortran and c-coding to use the goodies of 
|>both programming languages. For example I use a small c-program to allocate me
|>the memory dynamically I need in the Fortran main program.
|>
|>The following piece of code is a small Fortran test program which calls the 
|>c-routine to allocate memory. On the DEC-alpha this code bombs! However,
|>I manage to run the same code on SGI and IBM boxes. Is this problem related
|>to the OSF Unix?  I can't really understand this? I looked at the address
|>and it is transfered correctly to the Fortran subroutine but accessing the
|>memory location bombs the program. 

The problem is that Digital UNIX, unlike the other systems, is a 64-bit
address system and your code assumes that addresses fit in 32-bit integers.
I have modified the code as shown below and it works - my C is not the best
so there may be an alternative way of doing it (especially if you can declare
bv as a pointer to a pointer, since C knows what size that is.)  Note the
INTEGER*8 declaration in the Fortran code.

There's no need to use C for this purpose - Fortran provides malloc and
other routines in the 3f library.  See the DEC Fortran User Manual for
details.  DEC Fortran (and probably the others you use) supports the POINTER
statement which is much cleaner to use than the method you have here.

c........................ fortran part.
      Program test
      implicit double precision (a-h,o-z)
      integer*8 iaddress
      if = 8 * 1000
      call fmalloc(iaddress,if)
      call subrut(%val(iaddress))
      end

[remainder of Fortran code unchanged]

/*  c-part
     interface to a c routine to perform a dynamical
     memory allocation

     Leif Laaksonen (1992)

     Centre for Scientific Computing, Espoo, FINLAND
*/

#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <malloc.h>

/*
      bv:     is an integer to contain the address to the reserved
              memory

      reqlen: contains the number of bytes to be reserved
*/
/************************************************************************/
void  fmalloc_(bv , reqlen)
      long *bv;
      int *reqlen;
/************************************************************************/
{
      float *BV;

      BV = (float *)malloc(*reqlen);
      if(BV == NULL) {
       perror("Can't allocate memory");
       exit(1);}

      *bv = (long)BV;
}
/* ................................................................... */

