In Article <30BB7544.41C6@golay.med.unc.edu>
"Gernot M. Fuchs" <gfuchs@golay.med.unc.edu> writes:
>Hello,
>
>I'm experiencing problems with colour mapping on my DEC Alphas running
>OSF1 3.2 and Digital Unix respectively. When running Netscape on these
>machines, it seems there are too view colours available to display the
>graphics provided by the servers without dithering. Any other machine
>here (SGI, Linux-PC and SUN) does this properly with Netscape. 
>
>My question is: Am I doing something wrong with my X-Server, or is this
>a general limitation of the DEC X-Servers, or is this a Netscape
>Problem?

Netscape will grab almost all available color map slots if you don't override
this rude behavior.

netscape -install
will use a private color map. I use this, but many people find it unacceptable
because the colors on the rest of the windows will be wrong while Netscape is
the active window. (I don't care.)

netscape -ncol n
limits the number of colors Netscape grabs. An 8 bit color system will have 256
total colors. If you are not using something that uses a lot of colors, a value
of 128 is probably OK. It will rsult in minor color errors im some images in
Netscape.

