The folowing instruction is taken from http://www.w3.org/.... 
I think it answers Your question.

   
                               NOTES ABOUT OSF/1
                                       
   There are some problems with the W3C httpd running on an OSF/1 version
   3.0 platform. I have got this information from Per Steinar Iversen
   <PerSteinar.Iversen@fi.uib.no> which will integrate the W3C httpd
   better into OSF/1 3.0:
   
   The following little script eliminates this and integrates httpd
   better into OSF/1 V3.0:

#!/sbin/sh
#
# Start httpd.
#
PATH=/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
export PATH
#
NNTPSERVER=<your.news.server>
export NNTPSERVER
#
unset ftp_proxy
unset gopher_proxy
unset http_proxy
unset news_proxy
unset wais_proxy
umask 022
#
case "$1" in
'start')
        echo "Starting HTTPD..."
        /usr/local/www/httpd -r /etc/httpd.conf
        ;;
'stop')
        echo "Shutting down HTTPD..."
#       kill -9 `cat /usr/local/www/httpd-pid`
# Note: skill removes *all* httpd childen...
        /usr/local/bin/skill -9 httpd
        ;;
*)
        echo "usage: $0 {start|stop}"
        ;;
esac
#

   When the script is put into /sbin/init.d/httpd and have softlinks from
   /sbin/rc3.d/S97httpd and /sbin/rc3.d/K02httpd - then httpd
   starts/stops nicely integrated in the OSF/1 fashion, just like the DEC
   daemons does. "man rc3" for more info.
   
   Note the setting/unsetting of critical variables. Also the setting of
   umask - httpd just uses whatever root had at start-time, annoying for
   log-files.
   
   
     _________________________________________________________________
   
   NOTE We have recently received the following information from Eric R
   Schott <schott@zk3.dec.com> about the script itself and where to put
   it:
   
     * This example uses a local script that most Digital UNIX (aka DEC
       OSF/1) platforms don't have (reference to /usr/local/bin/skill)
     * It is correct to place it in /sbin/init.d
     * It is correct to soft link it to /sbin/rc3.d/S97httpd
     * It should NOT be linked as /sbin/rc3.d/K02httpd
     * INSTEAD, it should be soft linked as /sbin/rc0.d/K02httpd
     * AND it should be soft linked as /sbin/rc2.d/K02httpd
       
   
   
   
     _________________________________________________________________
   
   
    Henrik Frystyk, libwww@w3.org, July 1995


