In article <498la3$c7t@sundog.tiac.net>,
   slipcon@tiac.net (Scott Lipcon) wrote:

>acerint: overrun error; status = 63
>ace0: input silo overflow
>
>But I have figured one thing out about them... I slowed the modem and 
>serial port down to 19200, which all but stopped them.  I can use kermit, 
>netscape, telnet, etc... with NO errors, but as soon as I ftp anything, 
>it has tons of errors.  It was suggested to me that it might be because 
>ftp is binding ports.  Does this information help?

The error message is from the serial port device driver.  You'll see this 
printed whenever the serial port hardware receives characters but it's input 
buffer ("silo") is already full and the operating system hasn't serviced the 
port's interrupt to fetch the characters from the silo.

As you've already (almost) figured out, when you run the port at lower bit 
rates, these errors become less frequent.  Certain applications have the 
ability to cause these errors more easily due to the rate at which they pump 
data through the connection.  Tuning SLIP (for instance) may lower the 
frequency of these errors.  Hardware flow control will also help, but that 
feature won't be supported in driver until a later release of Digital UNIX.

Good luck,

