Vijay-

If you get some docs, please let me know.  I was running into similar
errors which finally ended up corrupting my system disk.  Apparently,
Digital Unix, unlike VMS, doesn't do automatic bad block replacement
on SCSI drives.  Basically, I saw a bunch of these types of errors,
which were apparently "Read recovered with ECC data" type errors, but
which finally turned into "Read failure" errors and would crash the
box whenever I tried to mount the volume.

My solution was to build a new system disk and use the SCU utility
to enable automatic bad block replacements on my SCSI drives.  This
seems to have fixed the problem.

Note for the Digital Unix folks:

If the drive reports a soft error when reading a block, would you
_please_ issue a replace block command?  Many drives (Seagate in
particular) come with automatic bad block replacement disabled.

If there is a way to enable this, please let me know!

Brett
wyer@telecheck.com



