lpz@nautique.epm.ornl.gov (Macintyre L P) writes:

>OK, I'll give you an example.  I've got a 3000/600 running 3.2A.  I was
>using the automounter, but after several days, it hangs.  I can't figure 
>a way to recover.

Certainly one of the problems with automount is trying to figure out
what's it's up to.  Look in daemon.log for hints (run automount with
-v to get more in daemon.log).  If you run it with options -T -T, it
will log to stdout all activity and is often the only way to figure
out what's up.  If you have an indirect map (e.g. /auto/net -hosts)
the superuser can turn logging off and on by referencing /auto/net/=0
and /auto/net/=2.  (Undocumented feature there....)

>  I have an application that runs out of a cron job and
>reads and writes a bunch of data on an NFS-mounted disk each 15 minutes.

>Once the automounter hangs, any access to any NFS-mounted disk causes a
>process to hang as well.

I assume you mean any automounted file system.  Manually mounted NFS systems
should keep working fine.
