In article <48f54k$bbr@hdxx05.telecom.ptt.nl>,
 <dirk@hdpd2124.iat_usr.telecom.ptt.nl> wrote:
>Like the subject says, I'm looking for information regarding
>Legato implementations on various platforms ( DEC UNIX,
>DEC OpenVMS, HP, SUN, SGI, ?).
>I'm currently involved in investigating/selecting backup
>products for UNIX mainly, but a beast that can do multiple
>platform backup would be ideal.
>Are there other (robust of course) products i can use ?

No problem using Legato for Solaris, with Solaris 2.4 boxes as
servers and clients.  It even backs up and restores OS/2 boxes,
and we've played with backups and restorations of Windows 3.1 and
Windows NT (client) machines.  Works pretty well.

Doesn't like restoring to OS/2 boxes when the backup is near the
end of a tape, but that could be our beat-up drive.  That's the
only problem we've had.  Oh, yes, one more problem., you can't
hot-swap one machine for another during backup, that is, you can't
restore a backup of 111.222.333.444 (cough) to 111.222.333.555
(heh) if both boxes are OS/2, but you can if they're Sparcs.

If going with Legato, consider bundling in the price of the [free]
software updates option.  Since we've been using it Legato's product,
the older version (about two years old now) didn't like Solaris
2.4 at all.  We used to back up and restore SunOS and Solaris boxes
from a SunOS server.  When we went to 2.4 from 2.3, suddenly Legato
didn't like us anymore, and we had to buy a new version instead of
doing a free upgrade.
