X-Deja-AN: 119823076
distribution: world
references: <48i16o$b9t@news.tuwien.ac.at>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: metsky@zk3.dec.com (David Metsky)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


From the engineer responsible for the loader:


The limit of roughly 95 shared libraries loaded either
as static dependencies or by dlopen is imposed by the
kernel configuration parameter for the maximum number
of mmap entries allowed per process.  This is set to
200 by default.  Each shared library requires at least 2
mmap entries (3 if it's bss size isn't zero -- use
"odump -ov" to identify the bss size.)  The program,
loader, heap and stack take up another 10-12 mmap entries.

As a rule of thumb, I'd use the formula

    mapentries = 20 + ( max-libraries * 3 )

To increase the maximum number of shared libraries that
a process can load simply reconfigure the kernel's
mapentries limit.  The following instructions will work on
DEC OSF/1 V3.2 and up.

To examine the current setting for the mapentries limit:

% /sbin/sysconfig -q vm vm-mapentries
vm-mapentries = 200

To change the mapentries limit to 1000:

1. edit /etc/sysconfigtab and add the following 2 lines

vm:
	vm-mapentries = 1000

2. reboot the system


-- 
David Metsky	Digital UNIX Software Group      metsky@zk3.dec.com
-- 
