X-Deja-AN: 119942006
references: <48t82d$la4@montego.umcc.umich.edu>
organization: The Natural Environment Research Council
reply-to: irt@unixa.nerc-keyworth.ac.uk
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hi

I have to say that our experiences with Legato have not been good.

We have been using a Solaris 2.3 Networker Server, with Sol. 2.3, SUNOS 4.1.3, SG IRIX
and OSF/1 clients. The OSF/1 clients are using POLYCENTER Networker Save and Restore,
while the other clients, and the Server, are using software supplied directly by
Legato, through a UK agent.

When we installed this, about 1 year ago, everything worked OK. However, in March, the
Alpha clients stopped working - overnight. We reported this to our supplier, then
DEC, and then Legato, and while we were waiting for a solution, tried to work out some
method of backing up our Alpha clients. NB - we had made no system changes of any kind.
No licenses had expired.

We haven't got a solution after SIX MONTHS. The problem has been passed around between
DEC, Legato, and our UK suppliers of Networker. We have had three different versions
of both Server and Client software, with no success.

We have managed to work out some fairly minimal backup cover, by using a 'save' command
which is part of the Networker product. We run this in cron. However, the software will
not cope with the difference between full and incremental backups - the only backup we
can do is a full one. The amount of data we need to back up off OSF/1 clients means
that we are unable to do a nightly full backup, owing to Network load, etc. Therefore,
we are limited to one full backup once per week, until we get our problem solved.

I have had to repeat sections of mail messages more than once to Legato support, and
have also been asked to run certain scripts more than once - when no changes to the
systems have taken place.

I've taken care to only list the facts of what have happened, as this is going out on
a public newsgroup - you must draw your own conclusions. I hope that this is of some
help to you in your work.

If you want to discuss this further, please feel free to email me at

i.talbot@ncs.nerc.ac.uk

Ian Talbot.





