I'm considering moving from an IBM RS/6000 w/AIX 3.2.5 platform to a
DEC 600 ev5/300 w/Digital UNIX 3.2C. I have an OS question.

Q: How stable is Digital UNIX 3.2C?

I'm a systems administrator with most of my experience in AIX 3.1 - 3.2.5.
I've read the press releases and FAQs for both AIX and Digital UNIX, and 
both seem to offer the same types of things (SMP, Multi-Threading, Journal
File systems, Logical Volume manager, Dynamic Linkable device drivers etc.)
BUT, I know through experience, what the  Press releases state and
how the OS actually performs in real life can be quite different.

Speaking for AIX (I have 4 years experience), versions 3.1.3 - 3.2.3 were
horrible! The systems would lock-up alot! IBM's patch scheme (PTFs) sucked.
They would send 100MB of patches to fix one bug, but other things would 
break. It wasn't until 3.2.5 that AIX stabalized. Of course no OS is perfect
but I can safely recommend AIX now. I wouldn't ever recommend versions
pre-3.2.5. Also, AIX 4.1.x seem to be just as stable.

Is Digital UNIX in the "acceptable" range or is it still in its 
"discovery stages"?
(i.e., do you have to add alot of patches to the Base OS?)

Anyone with experience in both AIX and Digital UNIX (aka. OSF/1)?

I'll continue to read the FAQs and newsgroups for more information
on the problems related to Digital UNIX, but any help would be appreciated.

