In article <48aa3t$9im@news.cea.fr>, francois CARTIER 
<cartier@lcus15.saclay.cea.fr> writes:
|>I have a C program calling Fortran functions, and inside the Fortran,
|>i need to call some C functions.
|>My programs comes from HPUX and i have to compile it on OSF1.
|>But the f77 compiler isn't very happy with my source file..
|>any help ?

In the current (V3.8) release of DEC Fortran, there's the CDEC$ ALIAS
directive which is similar to the $ALIAS directive in HP's Fortran, though
it specifies the name alias only, not the passing mechanism.  Your Fortran
code would look like this:


CDEC$ ALIAS recupfort,'recupfort'

     integer function essaifort()
     integer ii=123

     call recupfort(%val(ii))
     return
     end

The next version of Digital Fortran will include a new directive that will give
you the ability to say "this is the name of a C routine" and it will apply
C naming and passing-mechanism semantics automatically.
