>One of the classes in the department is having its students write a
>Pascal program.  In this program, they need the 'clock' function
> 
>This compiles fine, but fails miserably when trying to link:
> 
>ld:
>Unresolved:
>pas$clock2
>Pascal: Severe: Failed while trying to link.

Unfortunately, there was a regression in the Pascal RTL (its in the
DPORTLxxx subset) such that the pas$clock2 RTL routine was accidently
left out. (We changed our build around and forgot a -D on a 'cc' command...)

Here's the source for pas$clock2 and pas$sysclock2 (the 2 routines missing 
from DPORTL540)

#include <sys/resource.h>
long pas$clock2( void )
{

struct rusage r;

    getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF,&r);
    return ( (r.ru_utime.tv_sec*1000) + (r.ru_utime.tv_usec/1000) );

}

long pas$sysclock2( void )
{

struct rusage r;

    getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF,&r);
    return ( (r.ru_stime.tv_sec*1000) + (r.ru_stime.tv_usec/1000) );

}

Alternatively, you can go back an install an older DPORTLxxx subset.
There should be a DPORTL522 on the V3.2 CDROM itself (its near the '/'
directory, you'll have to look for it...)  You'll loose any other
bug fixes and new features (like support for QUADRUPLE) added to
DPORTL540 however...

A corrected DPORTLxxx will be included in the next release of Digital
UNIX as well as in the next compiler kit.  I'm planning on making a
'ECO' kit that should also be available from the support center as well
as Digital's ftp service (if I can figure out just how to do that).

