content-type: TEXT/PLAIN; charset=US-ASCII
organization: University of Maryland, Chesapeake Biological Lab.
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.security.unix,comp.unix.osf.osf1

I've configured anonymous ftp using wu-ftpd for my site.  I have two 
remaining questions, one general, one specific:

The way I set it up is a little too slick for my taste.  I _think_ my 
security bases are all covered in this setup, but I'm too paranoid to
accept that.  Have I overlooked something in the following configuration?

How do I set the UMASK for anon ftp users?  It is now 022, but I want 002
so that created subdirectories can be cleaned up by Joe LocalUser too.

- - -
Goals for Anonymous FTP security:
	1) Anon users be able to leave files in an incoming directory
	2) That they be able to see those files, but not fetch them
	3) Local users be able to delete those files
	4) Anon FTPers not be able to get a list of local uids

File permissions in my ftp directory:

drwxrwxr-x   8 root     archive      512 Nov 16 17:40 ./
dr-xr-xr-x   2 root     system       512 Nov 17 00:13 bin/
---s--x---   1 bin      ftp       409600 Jun 16 15:30 bin/ls*
dr-xr-xr-x   4 root     system       512 Nov 17 00:13 etc/
----r--r--   1 ftp      system        57 Nov 16 17:41 etc/group
----r--r--   1 ftp      system      6755 Nov 17 00:13 etc/passwd
drwxrwxr-x   2 ftp      users        512 Nov 17 01:48 incoming/
-rw-r--r--   1 root     system        38 Nov 16 16:48 incoming/README

So that our UIDs can't be seen by an anonymous ftp user, I
	1) created a passwd file which has our users in it
		(unneeded stuff filtered out, of course)
	2) set it owner/mode ftp.system/0044, so user ftp can't read it
	3) set /bin/ls to bin.ftp/4110, setuid so that it can access passwd
		as bin, and group readable so that local users can't use
		this ls to peek at bin's files elsewhere (some of which
		are sensitive)
	4) turned off chmod in /usr/local/etc/ftpaccess so user ftp
		can't change the mode of the passwd file.

