cflight@bcsc02.gov.bc.ca (Chris Flight) writes:
| Has anyone had any experiences trying to print from an OSF print queue	
| to a Windows NT based LPD? I keep running in the same messages:	
| 
| waiting for queue to be enabled on <hostname> 
| 
| The queue exists, and is enabled on the NT box but the OSF box can't seem	
| to forward the print streams (they just stay in the local spool).

i've seen this two times: when i forgot to make the unix spool directory
before trying to spool something, and when i changed something in printcap
that required kicking lpd (using the lpc 'restart' command).  other than
that, getting nt lpd going was fairly straightforward -- create the nt
printer, load and start lpdsvc, make the unix printcap entry and spools.

i've managed to get stuff from the unix box to the nt lpd, but now i have
a different problem.  the nt lpd is braindead -- the default lpd control 
characters that osf/1 puts into its control files make nt lpd think that
you want to print the job as text (i.e., line printer output).  you can
get around this by using "lpr -x", but you can only imagine how easy it
is to get people to remember to spool their postscript using an extra flag,
or use a script, or whatever.

i've seen some fairly complex solutions for this on alpha-osf-managers
(they involved multiple print spools, scripts with lpr commands invoked
from lpd filters, etc.) and frankly, i'd rather just have a way to make 
the osf/1 lpd use the 'x' (raw) control character for that printer by 
default.  another nice alternative would be a version of lpd for nt that
used the "%!PS" hack to figure out that postscript was coming.

any suggestions?



