QUESTION:  How does one make an RZ23, 24, 25, or 26 spin up at power up?

Hi there.  Can anyone shed any light on the following:  I have a collection
of RZ23, RZ24, RZ25, and RZ26L disk drives which I would like to use
on a system that requires them to spin up when power is applied.  At the
moment, as shipped, none of these disk types spinup on power up, instead
they wait for a SCSI START UNIT command.

Usually this is referred to in SCSI land as "motor start option", and is
controlled by a jumper on the drive's circuit board.  Generally one has to
install a jumper to keep the disk from spinning up when power is applied.
However,  I have tried every set of jumpers on these drives that I can
find, and none of them seem to enable the "spin up at power up" function.

I have heard rumors, however, that on these RZ drives this option is
controlled not by jumpers, but by firmware in the drive, and that the
behavior can be altered by use of software.  In particular, I have heard
of a UNIX or Ultrix-based program called RZSPINUP (as well as RZDISK),
which purport to be able to do this.

My question: HOW DO I GET THESE DRIVES TO SPIN UP?  Does anyone know where
a copy of this RZSPINUP command exists?  Does anyone know any alternative
way, hardware or software, to get these drives to spin up?

Any assistance, pointers, or advice will be GREATLY appreciated.

- John W. Manly  <JWMANLY@AMHERST.EDU>  (System Manager -- Amherst College)




