Subject: Re: TEST and SET/Spin lock for Alpha/Unix?
Date: 1995/11/18
Message-ID: <48jpti$pkl@agate.berkeley.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 119660310
references: <48imk7$j65@nntp.atlanta.com>
organization: University of California at Berkeley
reply-to: aoki@CS.Berkeley.EDU (Paul M. Aoki)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

curts@compgen.com (Curt Smith) writes:
| Anybody have the small bit of assembly it takes for a test and 
| set / spin lock in user space??

you might try using msem_lock(3).  it tries to use the LDL_L/STL_C 
load-linked/store-conditional instructions to obtain a semaphore 
from user mode before calling __msleep to sleep on the semaphore.
(if you use the MSEM_IF_NOWAIT flag, it returns immediately with 
an error instead of sleeping.)  furthermore, it gets the 
multiprocessor memory consistency protocol (i.e., use of the MB 
memory barrier instruction -- see the alpha architecture manual) 
right.

caveat 1: i have no idea how clustering affects all of this.  i've
only ever used standalone DS3000s and Sables.

caveat 2: before osf/1 3.0 (the first multiprocessor version), the 
msem_* routines *didn't* use the MB instruction.  hence, statically 
linking against pre-3.0 libraries is iffy if you are going to run 
the binaries on a multiprocessor.  however, things are fine >= 3.0.
-- 
