On Mon, 20 Nov 1995, Mark Thomas wrote:

> I think it is unwise for _any_ file or directory in the area to be owned
> by user "ftp". Also, I think you will probably want ftp to have read
> access to the etc/passwd file, so it can list owners of directories,

That's why ls is suid - so that ls can show user names instead of uids.
If ftp could read passwd, it could get the uids that way.  Not having the 
uids publicly visable was one of the goals.  Ill thought out... well, yes!
That's why I'm appealing to the Usenet public for comment.

> Also you might not let ftp have read access to your incoming directory.

I want remote users to be able to verify the size of files they have
uploaded to make sure they have used the right file transfer type, as well
as just seeing that they got there, since they can't verify the uploaded
files themselves.  I see the risk in nasty elements putting stolen
information in the *filenames*, but that is a more limited and more
noticable form of abuse. 

	Eric B.



