Just to add folks, I think I confused some people with the above voluming
terms. This may be because the terms are from Sequent softare identical to LSM
call SVM. Here's the clarification:

volume - highest level of a voluming device structure. This has a physical
	 device file (usually /dev/vol/???). This is the device you "newfs"
	 to create a UFS or "mkfdmn" and "mkfset" to create a ADVFS.

plex - the next layer down. A volume with multiple plexes is a software
       maintained mirror. ie. if a volume has three plexes, it is a three way
       mirror. If a volume has only one plex it is not mirrored at all.

subdisk - a subdisk is the lowest layer of the volume hierachy. It allows you
	  to map directly to the physical disks by specifying a disk device,
	  an offset and a length. "subdisks" are attached (logically) to a plex.
	  A plex may have many subdisks from completely separate physical disks,
	  thus the beauty of voluming S/W.

The terms associated with the Advanced filesystem stuff are:

domain - a pool of one or more disks. Unless you have the ADVFS tools (separate
	 license) that is all the functionality you get.

fileset - the equivalent of a filesystem. May only be created within an existing
	  domain.

Hence to make a 4Gb filesystem (must be AdvFs as UFS will not support that size)
you would simply:

1. create a "subdisk" on partition c (whole disk) of disk 1
2. create a "subdisk" on partition c of disk 2
3. create a plex and attach both subdisks to it
4. create a volume and attach the plex to it.
5. create a domain and a fileset on the volume.

Hope this does the trick. Sorry about the length of this post,

John

