Subject: Re: ROOT Security Login Problem
Date: 1995/11/12
Message-ID: <485qvm$387@louie.udel.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 119205812
references: <484gt2$bkc@spartacus.hula.net>
organization: University of Delaware, Newark
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <484gt2$bkc@spartacus.hula.net>, Cyber <cyber@hula.net> wrote:
>Question: Is it possible to disable ROOT logins by forcing all
>users to first login under their real user name and then make them
>SU to root.  At least here I would have a log entry of which user
>did the SU to root.  The system here is OSF 3.0 .

You can set up a file called /etc/securettys which explicitly defines which
ttys root can log in from. We have ours as follows:

    /dev/console
    local:0
    :0

That pretty much disables direct root logins from anywhere but the console.
Hence it forces users to log in as themselves and then su to root. Whether
this will work under v3.0 I don't know, as we've only been using v3.2.

Hope this helps!

David Carver
carver@eecis.udel.edu



