In <481oit$ckh@news2.deltanet.com> "Thumper!" <thumper@interceptor.com> writes:

>I found gcc-2.3.3 binaries (well, at least it's a start!), but every time I try
>and compile something, it's giving me an error that stdio.h is calling on a
>non-existent "standards.h"

The file has been there at least since OSF/1 2.0:

afref:~ 1> ls -l /usr/include/standards.h 
-r--r--r--   1 bin      bin         4433 Feb 22  1994 /usr/include/standards.h
afref:~ 2> uname -a
OSF1 afref.cern.ch V2.0 240 alpha

ues5:~ 138> ls -l /usr/include/standards.h 
-r--r--r--   1 bin      bin         4461 Feb  3  1995 /usr/include/standards.h
ues5:~ 139> uname -a
OSF1 ues5.cern.ch V3.2 17 alpha

Since your <stdio.h> is attempting to include it, it's safe to assume that
it should have been installed on your system, as well.  Unless you're
using a stdio.h from some gcc-specific directory which has not been 
generated by the gcc installation procedure on your system.  In this case,
all the bets are off.  Anyway, you can still force gcc to look for
header files in /usr/include and not in some other places.  Search the
options -I and -nostdinc in the gcc man page.

Dan
