Dave McCarthy (dave@vaxxine.com) wrote:
: We are experiencing frequent bus resets on our Alpha 3000/Model 300LX with
: our Fujitsu disk drives, model M2909S. We are running OSF 2.0. We recently
: added these drives to a system running on only DEC drives previously.
: Under heavy load the Fujitsu drives cause a bus reset as described in the
: log entry below. This causes the system to freeze up until the reset has
: completed ( a matter of a minute or so). If anyone else has encountered
: this problem and has a solution or suggestion it would be greatly
: appreciated. Please reply to dave@vaxxine.com or brad@vaxxine.com if you
: have any information.

Well, we have had something similar on a DEC 3000/300L.  We are running
OpenVMS, so the error log is not the same, and I didn't really see the
things I'm used to seeing.  We were using Exabyte 8505 tapedrives in a
TTI box, and when we loaded both drives with double-buffered I/O, we
would get 'command transfer incomplete', I think.  Then the SCSI bus
would reset.  We sometimes got the same sort of bus lockup on another
300L which only had disk drives on it, when we ran a multi-stream I/O
program on that disk.  In other words, when there were too many transfers
per second, the SCSI bus had a problem.  DEC didn't know anything about
it, and we were pretty desperate.  The same drives worked fine and ran
flat out on a 3000 / 400, so we really suspected firmware, port drivers
or hardware on the system board.  The problem was finally solved when we
reloaded the drives with a newer firmware release, and put updated
ROMs in the controller in the TTI boxes.  We still see the disk hang
about once every 3-6 months, which is probably acceptable here.  Anyway,
its pretty hard to track down something that only fails every 3 months.
So, our problem was firmware in the tape drive or box malfunctioning
when several drives were transferring at a very high rate.  We use all
non-DEC disk drives here, and so there could be some very obscure firmware
problem in those drives, too.

I don't know if this helps, but it is at least what we experienced.




