From: privitt@eagle.tamu.edu (Carl Privitt)
Subject: Re: Compile failure: Elm v2.4pl24 & DEC OSF/1 v3.2c
Date: 1995/11/06
Message-ID: <47l364$pe8@news.tamu.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118655055
references: <47bkk9$i5f@news.tamu.edu> <47bj4u$ha8@news.tamu.edu> <47d305$bfk@usenet.ucs.indiana.edu>
followup-to: comp.mail.elm
summary: Can't compile Elm under OSF/1
organization: Texas A&M University, College Station
newsgroups: comp.mail.elm,comp.unix.osf.osf1

Evidently, Configure does not correctly determine the signal return type
under DEC OSF/1 v3.2c.  It thinks signal() returns an int when, in fact,
it returns a void.  The solution is to edit config.sh at the end of
Configure's questions (when it suggests using a shell escape).  Change

    sigtype='int'

to be

   sigtype='void'

I successfully compiled Elm v2.4pl24 with DEC's native C compiler using just
the default flags (-O) and with the more strict ansi flags (-migrate -std1).

Thanks to John Steele <steele@tamu.edu>, David Tuttle <dct@odin.mdacc.tmc.edu>,
and Alec Habig <ahabig@bigbang.astro.indiana.edu> who all responded with the
correct fix.

-- 
