In article <47r3dj$v9h@minerva.worldbank.org>, Bassirou Bah 
<bbah@worldbank.org> writes:
>Hi:
>
>We are about to install an HSZ40-BD controller and later on
>replace it with an HSZ40-BF  to handle a RAID 1 config.
>The thinking is that we want to mirror the system disk for
>maximum system availability.
>
>The question is. Does it make sense to install the system disk
>from on an HSZ40-BD/BF controller. Will it be possible to boot
>without problem?
>Has anyone ever setup this configuration?
>

From my experience of HSZ40 devices if you were going to put your system 
disk on one, either as a single disk or a shadow set, you must make this 
unit 0 otherwise the console cannot see it and you will not be able to boot.
This restriction may not apply on all machines but it does on DEC 7000 
machines as the console firmware does not recognise the SCSI LUNS that the 
HSZ40 puts on the bus. Once the Operating System has got the machine then
things are O.K. as OSF (and VMS for that matter) can see the LUNS even 
though they are not seen at console level via a show dev command.

I have an HSZ40 serving disks to a DEC 8400 running OSF and it works fine.
I haven't tried booting off it though.
 

