Scott Lipcon (slipcon@tiac.net) wrote:
: 	probably... I have a question and a problem relating to that 
: though... in /usr/sys/conf/ there is a file called JENSEN01, which *was* 
: the name of the system when the kernel was built.  I didn't set the 
: system up, so one of the first things I did when I got it was to change 
: the name to "thira".... Should I copy JENSEN01 to THIRA, or move it, or 
: does it not matter?

At this point it probably wouldn't make any different.  When you built
the kernel you have two basic choices.  One lets it create a new
configuration file, which will have the name of your system.  This is
good when you add hardware and the like since it should get everything
set correctly.  The second involves you're telling it what
configuration file to use, in which case you should be able to use
whatever name you want.  This is nice if you're just adding an option
or two.  Particularly if you've modified the conf file in the past,
you don't want to lose those changes by rebuilting the kernel from
scratch.

: 	THe problem... this system was donated by Digital, and it managed 
: NOT to come with ANY documentation!  

As they use to say when I was in the army, you're in deep kimchi.
Didn't you say you had the online doc?  I don't like online doc, but
most of the info is there.

: Here is the 'df' output:

: Filesystem   512-blocks        Used       Avail Capacity  Mounted on
: /dev/rz1a        126462      117828           0   104%    /
: /proc                 0           0           0   100%    /proc
: /dev/rz1g        792124      570908      142002    80%    /usr
: /dev/rz1h        810952       22860      706996     3%    /var
: /dev/rz0c       1010602       21512      888028     2%    /extra

I've seen PCs with more disk space.

You might want to do a "du / | sort -nr -2 | more" to get a sorted
list, high to low, of where you disk space is going.  This could take
awhile. It will give you results both at the level of directories and
files.  Look for large accounting files and/or directories that could
be moved the /extra and linked to from the current location.
Something below your top level is eating up the space in the / partition.

