In article <DHBK3I.Mp9@rtpnc.epa.gov>, luis@itzamn.ath.epa.gov (Luis A. Suarez)
 writes:
|>My question is: how can I make STDERR to honor fortran carriage control
|>information and i/o redirection at the same time?

Luis had also sent this question to me by mail.  My answer was as follows:

The answer is yes and no.  The "file" name must be omitted in order to
redirect the output to STDERR.  And indeed this program works when the
file name is omitted; however, the output file will appear as follows:

+default STDERR
+default STDERR
+default STDERR
+default STDERR
+default STDERR
redefined STDERR^Mredefined STDERR^Mredefined STDERR^MredefinedSTDERR^Mredefined STDERR^M

Note that the carriage controls (^M) are written to the file as raw 
characters as the file does not know if/how to format it.  If this same
file was then output to a terminal ("cat w.del") it would then appear
as you wish ("redefined STDERR" would be overstruck).
