harnold@du.edu (hugh arnold) wrote:
>I am running DU3.1+ some patches on an Alpha 2100.  About once a month and
>a half or so, the system locks up.  To be more honest, it seems to have filled
>its process table.  fork's are disallowed.  Generally, I keep the system
>pretty zombie free, and I assume no single user could be responsible.  
>About which, where do I find a list of all the kernel options. 
I left Digital Unix world last July... but here is what I
remember.

The parms to build your kernel can be found in the directory
/usr/sys/conf in a file name after the machine name.  They are
described in your System Admin Guide or the Installation Guide.

You can set the maximum number of processes a given user can
start by setting the "maxuprc" (or maxu procs or maxuprcs)
parm.

The total number of processes, i,e. the size of the process
table, is normally derived from the value of the "maxusers"
parm.  However you can set it explicitly by setting the
"maxprocs" parm.


> Can I restrict
>the amount of memory a single user can use?  The number of processes they
>run et cetera?  
>
Other parameters can limit the the max process size, etc... but it is 
not a good idea to set it too low because all processes in the system 
are then limited to that size.  Depending on the shell your users are
using you can set "limits" in logging scripts.

