Scott Lipcon (slipcon@max.tiac.net) wrote:
: On Tue, 31 Oct 1995, Amy Lenox wrote:
: >   One place where I wouldn't scrimp - don't move genvmunix
: > out of the root partition.  Do make your current vmunix
: > as small as possible.  

And check whether you have a vmunix.old lying around. You may have
this if you've rebuilt your kernel at some point. This is nice to
keep in case your new kernel has a problem, but it takes up a lot of
space and you have the genvmunix to fall back on anyway.

: 	genvmunix is only 5 or 6 Mb I think, and my vmunix is about 9.  I 
: think that is pretty big, but I haven't had time to figure out how to 
: customize it (I am a beginner at this!) 

Go into the directory /usr/sys/conf.  There should be a file with the
same name as your system that contains basic configuration
information.  Check out the doc, chapter 5 of the "System
Administration" book that came with dUnix.  There's various options
you probably don't need.

:genvmunix is only 5 or 6 Mb I think, and my vmunix is about 9.  I
:think that is pretty big, but I haven't had time to figure out how to
:customize it (I am a beginner at this!)

Welcome to the wonderful world of Unix system admin where there are
three kinds of people, gurus, novices, and novices pretending to be
gurus.  It's a little like the days of alchemy where if you wanted to
learn the cult secrets you had to sit at the foot of a master and wait
for him to impart wisdom. (If I sound bitter it's because I became a
sysadmin at a site with no guru which meant learning as I went.  This
is a little like OJT as an astronaut).

: What is the maxusers?  Is that 
: the max that can be logged on?  

No.  It's a table size and not very important.  Reducing it might save
you some space, but not enough to write home about.

: 	I made one account in /usr/users/ before I realized that if I did 
: that then my /usr partition would fill up too quickly (its at %80 now, I 
: think their is about 80 or 90Mb free) and started creating accounts in 
: /extra/home/, which is almost empty (2%).  There are only 5 user accounts 
: right now, so that is not the problem. 

Sounds like you just need some bigger disks. Or some more disks.
Depending on how your filesystems are set up now, there is probably
something you can move to another partition and just mount in /.



