We have a dial up xyplex terminal server that supports LAT and TCP.

It supports lots of connections to lots of hosts.

There is a problem with LAT connections to a particular host, though, 
making me think the problem lies with the host, and not with the
terminal server.

Lots of connections to this host, a 3000/500 ALPHA with OSF 3.2 on it, 
hang. By that I mean that lots of people who login to this host
via the LAT terminal server still show up in the output of ``w'' days
later, with the appropriate amount of idle time accrued. The problem,
of course, is that these people left, logged out, turned off their modems,
whatever, days ago. From the terminal server's perspective, the 
associated ports are still allocated (and therefore unusable by others) 
and connected to this host. When I single out one of these ``idle''
users and kill their processes, they go <defunct>. The 
terminal server manager has to go detectiving around every so often
collecting up these ``idle'' osf users and manually log out their
ports on the terminal server.

As I said, I'd like to believe that since this problem only involves
this particular host, that the problem lies here instead of on the 
terminal server. 

Has anyone seen anything like this? Anyone know what to do?

-Mike



