I sure do like OSF/1 and ALPHAs, but...

After I got through upgrading to the most recent release of the OS
I started seeing that the kernel would grow to ludicrous size within
a few days and performance would hit the skids.

Turns out that was caused by a kernel memory leak associated with heavy
LAT traffic, and a kernel patch from DEC fixed the problem. 

Now that the machine can run for a long time between reboots, another
problem that showed up after the upgrade is starting to thwart us.

Unkillable (I don't mean they go <defunct> when they receive a -9,
I mean that *nothing* happens) ntalkd's stack up until the
machine runs out of processes (No More Processes). The other day,
after the machine had been up for a couple of weeks, there were a
hundred or so people logged on, and everyone was started getting
``No More Processes'', instead of results, when they'd hit enter.
A quick pipe of ps into grep and then wc showed that there were almost
*700* of these brain dead ntalkds cluttering things up.

Is there a known good fix for this?

-Mike



